// `Map.entries()` leaked three buffers per call.
//
// Originally 28 bytes/iteration over a one-entry `Map<string, i32>`:
// `emit_map_entries` calls `emit_collection_array` twice internally (once
// for keys, once for values) to build its own temporary arrays, then builds
// a third, fresh array of `[K, V]` tuples by copying each field *by value*
// out of those two, and none of the three was ever claimed.
//
// Closed in two passes. First, the two internal arrays: each tuple's fields
// are a plain value copy (`build_load` then `build_insert_value`), so a key
// that is itself an allocation ends up with two pointers to the same one,
// the slot inside `keys`, and the copy just placed in a tuple. Only the
// *buffer* that held those pointers belongs to `emit_map_entries`; the
// pointers themselves moved into the tuples. Freeing the `keys`/`values`
// buffers alone, right after the copy loop, closed those two without
// touching what the tuples now alias.
//
// Second, the outer tuple array itself, plus what it aliases: registered
// with the default `Deep` plan rather than a flat buffer-only one, which
// routes through the async drop-glue system (`async_owned_value_supported` /
// `emit_async_drop_value_in_place`) once a type qualifies. Neither had a case
// for `Type::Tuple` before, this is the first caller to build one composed
// of an owned element (a `string` key), so both gained one, positional
// rather than named, mirroring the existing `Type::Named`/`Type::Object`
// field-walking arm exactly: skip a `Copy` element, `build_struct_gep` and
// recurse into the rest. The array-of-non-Copy-element path already existing
// in `emit_async_drop_value_in_place` (built for `Type::Array(Type::Str)`,
// among others) then walks the tuple array correctly with no further change,
// freeing each tuple's owned key and the array's own buffer. Confirmed with
// valgrind reporting zero heap blocks in use at exit, not just zero bytes
// "definitely lost", and confirmed a returned entry's key is still valid
// (not a dangling alias into a buffer freed out from under it) by reading it
// back after the call.
const main = (): void => {
    const shared = Map<string, i32>();
    shared.set("a", 1);
    let total: usize = 0;
    for (let i: i32 = 0; i < 200; i += 1) {
        total += shared.entries().length;
    }
    console.log(total);
    console.log("assert:", 200);
};
